<h2>From FIR to closure in thirteen months</h2>
<p>The marriage was solemnised on 4 December 2024. By October 2025 the complainant had alleged pre-marriage demands — a residential property, a car, expensive watches — harassment and assault for insufficient dowry, and wrongful retention of her jewellery, leading to the FIR against the husband and his parents. What followed is the now-familiar settlement arc: an MOU-cum-settlement deed on 23 December 2025; a mutual-consent divorce under Section 13B(2) HMA decreed on 23 May 2026; and a quashing petition in which the complainant appeared, confirmed the settlement&rsquo;s voluntariness before the Court and the investigating officer, and received the final bank draft of Rs. 3.5 lakh in the courtroom itself, completing the agreed Rs. 7 lakh.</p>

<h2>The settlement-quashing checklist, as applied</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>A written settlement with a schedule.</strong> The MOU fixed the total figure and its tranches; the final payment was synchronised with the quashing hearing, which is standard protective practice for both sides.</p>
  <p><strong>Divorce completed, not merely promised.</strong> The 13B(2) decree predated the quashing, removing any suggestion that the FIR was being traded for an unperformed promise.</p>
  <p><strong>The complainant&rsquo;s free confirmation.</strong> Verified in open court and through the IO — the safeguard that distinguishes a genuine compromise from a procured one.</p>
  <p><strong>No public-interest element.</strong> The Court recorded that the FIR involved no heinous offence, and that keeping it alive would only perpetuate hostility between former spouses.</p>
</div>

<h2>The authorities the Court applied</h2>
<p>The judgment rests on the settled quartet. <strong>Gian Singh</strong> (para 58) recognises the High Court&rsquo;s power to quash non-compoundable offences arising from family and matrimonial disputes upon settlement. <strong>Narinder Singh</strong> (para 29) structures the discretion. <strong>Jitendra Raghuvanshi</strong>, (2013) 4 SCC 58, puts it in terms the Court quoted: <em>&ldquo;it is the duty of the courts to encourage genuine settlements of matrimonial disputes.&rdquo;</em> And recent applications — <strong>Navneesh Aggarwal v. State of Haryana</strong>, 2025 SCC OnLine SC 1680, and the coordinate-bench decision in <strong>Shamsher Singh v. State</strong>, 2026 SCC OnLine Del 7626 — show the same approach operating on post-divorce dowry FIRs. The operative conclusion was unqualified: the FIR and all proceedings arising from it <em>&ldquo;stand quashed&rdquo;</em> as against all three petitioners.</p>

<h2>Why BNS-era numbering deserves attention</h2>
<p>This is among the growing set of quashings where the FIR itself is registered under the BNS. Practitioners drafting settlement deeds and quashing petitions should plead the BNS sections as registered and map them to their IPC predecessors, since the settled 498A jurisprudence — from Gian Singh to the Delhi template orders — applies with equal force to Section 85 BNS. Mismatched numbering between the MOU, the divorce pleadings and the quashing petition remains a common, avoidable registry objection.</p>
<div class="note">Quashing on settlement is discretionary and fact-specific; it does not follow automatically from payment or divorce. Case details are as recorded in the judgment dated 17 November 2026.</div>
